license: MIT
version: 0.3.0
date-released: 2026-11-13
# The concept DOI stays here, because it always resolves to the latest version
# and that is what a citation of the software should point at. The version DOI
# below it is 0.2.0's, assigned by Zenodo on 2026-10-10. This version, 0.3.0,
# is cut and NOT archived -- archiving is the owner's act -- so it has no
# version DOI yet, and the one below is labelled with the version it belongs to
# rather than relabelled as this one's. That relabelling is the error this
# block already caught once, recorded below.
#
# 0.2.1 WAS NEVER RELEASED. It was cut on 2026-11-10, tagged locally, and the
# release was never made; four commits then added the fifth job (fixing some
# sectors' output and solving for the rest) and the sixth (the multiplier
# table). A snapshot at that tag would be missing both, so this freeze is cut
# at 0.3.0 instead. The v0.2.1 tag is left where it is because it is a real
# point in the history, not relabelled.
#
# THIS IS THE FROZEN SNAPSHOT. Decided by the owner on 2026-11-13: the public
# repository stops being a continuously mirrored tree and becomes a citable
# release. See `COMO_PUBLICAR.md`.
#
# What must not happen, and nearly did: the entry that stood here before the
# bump was v0.1.0's (10.5281/zenodo.22345638). Leaving it would have relabelled
# an earlier version's DOI as this one's, and a Zenodo record cannot be
# corrected afterwards.
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22345637
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22345637
    description: "Concept DOI — resolves to the latest version"
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.23283923
    description: "Version DOI — version 0.2.0, archived 2026-10-10. This version, 0.2.1, has no version DOI until it is archived; the concept DOI above resolves to whatever is latest"
abstract: >-
  An engine for building and manipulating supply-use and input-output systems:
  loading published tables from statistical offices, disaggregating sectors
  against proxy data, balancing with RAS and GRAS, transforming supply-use
  pairs into symmetric tables, projecting to a later year, regionalising a
  national table with the location quotient family, estimating how much of a
  region's trade is two-way with the commodity balance and CHARM, which the
  quotient family cannot give because a net trade position erases the imports
  and exports that cancel out, and loading any region of
  the European multiregional archive with the rest of its country and of Europe
  as further blocks, with Eurostat's regional employment attached. Every method
  is implemented against the paragraph and page of the manual that specifies
  it, every cell carries whether it was observed, estimated or adjusted, and
  every result is printed together with the measured cost of the assumptions
  that produced it.
keywords:
  - input-output analysis
  - supply-use tables
  - regionalisation
  - multiregional input-output
  - location quotient
  - cross-hauling
  - sector disaggregation
  - reproducible research
